 class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"User:Mich.kramer\">Michael Kramer</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 3.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"51265102":{"pageid":51265102,"ns":6,"title":"File:Team Tomato (6888) (8755613486).jpg","index":13,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":3724155,"width":2784,"height":1848,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Team_Tomato_%286888%29_%288755613486%29.jpg/500px-Team_Tomato_%286888%29_%288755613486%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":400,"thumbheight":266,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Team_Tomato_%286888%29_%288755613486%29.jpg/960px-Team_Tomato_%286888%29_%288755613486%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Team_Tomato_%286888%29_%288755613486%29.jpg/500px-Team_Tomato_%286888%29_%288755613486%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":400,"height":266,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bc/Team_Tomato_%286888%29_%288755613486%29.jpg/960px-Team_Tomato_%286888%29_%288755613486%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bc/Team_Tomato_%286888%29_%288755613486%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Team_Tomato_(6888)_(8755613486).jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=51265102","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"<p>Microbiologist Rebecca Bell examines data to see which tomato samples test positive for  <i>Salmonella</i> DNA. Tomatoes are vulnerable to contamination by <i>Salmonella</i>, bacteria that are a common cause of foodborne illness. At FDA, there is a group of researchers nicknamed \u201cTeam Tomato\u201d that tries to understand and prevent <i>Salmonella</i> contamination early in the tomato production process, working with state extension services, growers, and industry. To learn more about Team Tomato read this Consumer Update <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.fda.gov/ForConsumers/ConsumerUpdates/ucm359658.htm\">\"FDA's Team Tomato Fights Contamination.\"</a>\n</p><p>To learn more about how FDA is using genetic research to help ensure food safety read this Consumer Update: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.fda.gov/ForConsumers/ConsumerUpdates/ucm397287.htm\">Whole Genome Sequencing Helps FDA Identify Dangerous Bacteria</a>. \n</p><p>This photo is free of all copyright restrictions and available for use and redistribution without permission. Credit to the U.S. Food and Drug Administration is appreciated but not required.\n</p><p>Privacy and use information: <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/fdaphotos/\">www.flickr.com/people/fdaphotos/</a>\n</p>\nFDA photo by Michael J. Ermarth","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2013-04-10 12:37","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.flickr.com/people/39736050@N02\">The U.S. Food and Drug Administration</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]}}}}